{
  "segment_function": "To analyze how political philosophy historically attempted to escape the frailty and uncertainties of action through monocratic rule and the philosophical division between ruling (archein) and executing (prattein).",
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The traditional escape from the frailty of human affairs into stability is characterized by the concept of rule, which assumes that political community requires some to command and others to obey.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "Escape from the frailty of human affairs into the solidity of quiet and order has in fact so much to recommend it that the greater part of political philosophy since Plato could easily be interpreted as various attempts to find theoretical foundations and practical ways for an escape from politics altogether. The hallmark of all such escapes is the concept of rule, that is, the notion that men can lawfully and politically live together only when some are entitled to command and the others forced to obey.",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "qualifications": [],
      "confidence": 1
    },
    {
      "claim": "Plato introduced a division between those who know and do not act and those who act and do not know, separating the initiative of beginning (archein) from the execution (prattein).",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "Plato was the first to introduce the division between those who know and do not act and those who act and do not know, instead of the old articulation of action into beginning and achieving, so that knowing what to do and doing it became two altogether different performances.",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "qualifications": [],
      "confidence": 1
    }
  ],
  "definitions": [],
  "distinctions": [
    {
      "item_a": "archein (\"beginning\")",
      "item_b": "prattein (\"achieving\" or executing)",
      "distinction": "Plato opened a gulf between these two modes of action, which were traditionally interconnected, separating the initiative of the beginner (who rules) from the labor of those who execute orders."
    }
  ],
  "references_to_other_sections": [],
  "unresolved_context": [],
  "must_not_be_lost": [
    "The connection between the human condition of plurality, the public realm, and the various forms of one-man rule or monarchy as attempts to eliminate plurality.",
    "The interpretation of the history of political philosophy since Plato as an attempt to find a substitute for action rather than a tyrannical will to power."
  ]
}

